  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 945 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat and humidity will persist today with \nanother slight chance of showers and thunderstorms in the late \nafternoon/evening. \n\n\nMorning weather analysis shows zonal flow aloft, while at the \nsurface the Bermuda high continues to remain off the SE coast. With \nthe high pressure off the coast it is continuing to keep \ntemperatures early this morning quite mild with low to mi 70s inland \nand upper 70s to low 80s along the coast. Through the rest of the \nmorning not expecting temperatures to drop but perhaps a degree or \ntwo as dew points also remain elevated. Through the day the heat and \nhumidity will persist. Temperatures are likely to rise into the\nmid 90s for most by mid-afternoon. These temperatures combined \nwith dew points in the low to mid 70s will allow for another \nday of heat indices in the upper 90s to low 100s (most around\n100F). However, the heat indices will likely fall short of Heat\nAdvisory criteria. In terms of storm chances for this \nafternoon, there are two areas of interest that have the best \nchance for showers and thunderstorms. The first is across the \nfar SE where the sea-breeze has a slight chance of firing an \nisolated shower and or thunderstorm this afternoon. The next \narea is across the far north. Models guidance shows a weak \nshortwave moving aloft that could potentially spark showers and \nthunderstorms along a remnant outflow boundary from the \nconvection to the west. However, there continues to remain a \nhigh amount of uncertainty, so decided to leave a slight chance\nof pops (15-25%). The greatest threat with any storm that \npotentially initiates is very localized flash flooding and \ndamaging winds. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A more unsettled pattern returns Tuesday through Thursday. \nHowever, there continues to remain quite a bit of uncertainty.\n\nThe recent 00z/10 ensembles are in decent agreement on the upcoming \nsynoptic weather pattern. The zonal flow pattern is expected to \nbreak down as a ridge aloft starts to build in Tuesday across the \ncentral United States. This will allow for WNW flow aloft to return \nto the area. While at the surface, the Bermuda high will start to \nbreak down slightly and move over the the Gulf states. This will \nallow the possibility for multiple days of  active weather (Tues-\nThurs) to return to the area. However, there continues to remain \nquite a bit of uncertainty at this time due to some slight \ndisagreement in the models. The synoptic pattern does look \nfavorable, however, will need to watch real time trends as the \nconvection evolves west of the area during this time frame. The 00z \nHigh-res and Medium range CAMs are starting to show subsidence over \nthe area Tuesday afternoon as a possible decaying MCS moves over the \narea. This will allow convection to struggle to initiate that day. \nHowever, if the MCS takes longer to evolve out west and moves over \nthe CWA during the afternoon then the severe threat will increase. \nWednesday and Thursday are similar situations as Tuesday and all \nthree days will play off of each other. Nonetheless, uncertainty on \nthe severe threat remains. If severe weather does evolve the main \nthreat will be damaging wind and localized flooding. \n\nIn addition to the storm chances, the heat and humidity will persist \nTues-Thurs. Tuesdays highs will likely be in the low to middle 90s. \nAt this time not expecting dew points to mix and should remain in \nthe low to middle 70s. The confidence for any potential heat \nheadlines continues to decrease as there remains uncertainty with \nthe potential convection. For Wed and Thurs temperatures have \ntrended slightly cooler with upper 80s along the MD Eastern shore \nand low 90s but depending on convection and cloud cover they could \nbe slightly cooler. Nonetheless, heat indices will potentially \nbe in the upper 90s to low 100s as dew points remain in the 70s.\nBy the end of the week, ensembles hint on a cold front pushing \nthrough the area that could help lower temperatures and dew \npoints across the area.",
